        "description": "<div id=\"link_wrapped_content\">\n<body><section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p>In the new Chrome versions 147.0.7727.116/117 for Windows and macOS and 147.0.7727.116 for Linux, the developers have fixed 19 security vulnerabilities. According to Google, none of these vulnerabilities are being exploited in the wild.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>In the <a href=\"https://chromereleases.googleblog.com/2026/04/stable-channel-update-for-desktop_22.html\" data-type=\"link\" data-id=\"https://chromereleases.googleblog.com/search/label/Stable updates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Chrome Releases blog post</a>, Srinivas Sista lists only vulnerabilities that were discovered and reported by external security researchers. That amounts to a mere three out of 19 vulnerabilities. Two are classified as high risk: the use-after-free vulnerability (<a href=\"https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-6919\">CVE-2026-6919</a>) is found in the DevTools component. A memo",
